%S A400663 0,3,40,214,830,2355,6141,13566,27369,51449,91630,152580,249790,
%T A400663 386793,580727,859025,1239949,1733260,2408943,3251493,4355095,5767041,
%U A400663 7525777,9640330,12359293,15598888,19513469,24291386,29936448,36474044,44568762,53739809,64488463
%N A400663 a(n) is the number of congruence classes of nondegenerate triangles whose vertices are points of an n X n X n grid.
%C A400663 Here an n X n X n grid consists of the (n + 1)^3 integer points (i, j, k) with 0 <= i, j, k <= n.
%C A400663 A congruence class is uniquely determined by its sorted triple of squared side lengths.
%C A400663 Let c_n = {(r^2, s^2, (r - s)^2): r, s are integers and max(0, r, s) - min(0, r, s) <= n}. Its elements are exactly the possible contributions of one coordinate to the three squared side lengths, and |c_n| = 3*n*(n + 1)/2 + 1.
%C A400663 For h(x, y, z) = 2*(x*y + x*z + y*z) - x^2 - y^2 - z^2, a squared side-length triple (x, y, z) is nondegenerate iff h(x, y, z) > 0.
%C A400663 The triple (n^2, 2*n^2, 3*n^2) occurs first at level n, so a(n) is strictly increasing for n >= 1.
%C A400663 Good Claude Hunting independently computed a(0), ..., a(7) in April 2026.
%H A400663 Felix Huber, <a href="/A400663/b400663.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..42</a>.
%H A400663 Felix Huber, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23158188">Congruence Classes of Triangles in a Cubical Grid</a>.
%H A400663 Felix Huber, <a href="/A400663/a400663.pdf">Illustration of the term a(2) = 40</a>.
%H A400663 Felix Huber, <a href="/A400663/a400663_1.txt">Maple program</a>.
%H A400663 Good Claude Hunting, <a href="https://good-claude-hunting.vercel.app/discoveries/3d-lattice-triangles-by-angle">3D Lattice Triangles Have a Specific Angle-Class Distribution Under Bounded Vertices</a> (independent computation of a(0) through a(7), with indexing shifted by one).
%F A400663 a(n) = #{sort([x, y, z]): (x, y, z) = c_1 + c_2 + c_3, c_1, c_2, c_3 in c_n, h(x, y, z) > 0}.
%F A400663 A028419(n) <= a(n) <= binomial(3*n*(n + 1)/2 + 3, 3).
%F A400663 a(n) > a(n - 1) for n >= 1.
%F A400663 a(n) = Theta(n^6/sqrt(log(n))).
%F A400663 Limit_{n -> infinity} (log(a(n)) - 6*log(n))/log(log(n)) = -1/2.
%e A400663 a(0) = 0 since the grid consists of a single point.
%e A400663 a(1) = 3. The three congruence classes have squared side-length triples (1, 1, 2), (1, 2, 3), and (2, 2, 2).
%e A400663 a(2) = 40; see the illustration in the links.
%p A400663 # See Huber link.
%Y A400663 Cf. A028419, A028492, A103426, A103428, A103429, A103499, A189979, A190021, A190022, A241231, A241232, A241233, A241234.
